{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-47679" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that that a function $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=1$, has a jump discontinuity at $x=2$, and  is differentiable at $x=3$, which of the following statements must be true for **any** $f(x)$ that satisfy these criteria?\n\n- It is differentiable at $x=1$\n- It  is **not** differentiable at $x=2$\n- It is continuous at $x=3$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/state.json" "file_sha256" "c528af12e32c51bb13e2c6a2c8187de963ee2bc86d9d5b36574231eba23d0e7f"} {"question" "q-47679" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "- Statement I is false. Continuity does not imply differentiability. For example, the function $y=|x-1|$ is continuous but it's not differentiable at $x=1.$\n\n- Statement II is true.  A function that is discontinuous at a point can't be differentiable at this point (because differentiability implies continuity).\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, differentiability implies continuity." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/state.json" "file_sha256" "c528af12e32c51bb13e2c6a2c8187de963ee2bc86d9d5b36574231eba23d0e7f"} {"question" "q-47679" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3fc0483942561db3ea45757c1e71d91075bb66bcb91621aa8b7a809a03e90641"} {"question" "q-47679" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/state.json" "file_sha256" "c528af12e32c51bb13e2c6a2c8187de963ee2bc86d9d5b36574231eba23d0e7f"} {"question" "q-47679" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and  III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/state.json" "file_sha256" "c528af12e32c51bb13e2c6a2c8187de963ee2bc86d9d5b36574231eba23d0e7f"} {"question" "q-47679" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and  III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/state.json" "file_sha256" "c528af12e32c51bb13e2c6a2c8187de963ee2bc86d9d5b36574231eba23d0e7f"} {"question" "q-47686" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions is not differentiable over the interval $(-1,1)$ due to the existence of a vertical asymptote at a point in this interval?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/state.json" "file_sha256" "c528af12e32c51bb13e2c6a2c8187de963ee2bc86d9d5b36574231eba23d0e7f"} {"question" "q-47686" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function $y=g(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and is  therefore not differentiable at $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$ So $y=g(x)$ is the correct answer.\n\nThe other graphs do not have a a vertical asymptote inside the interval $(-1,1).$\n\n- The function $y=p(x)$ has a corner point at $x=-\\frac{1}{2}$ and is therefore not differentiable at $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The function $y=h(x)$ has a vertical tangent at $x=0$ and is therefore not differentiable at $x=0.$\n\n- The function $y=q(x)$ is smooth and continuous over the entire interval $x\\in (-1,1)$ and is therefore differentiable at every point in the interval.\n\n- The function $y=f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=\\frac{1}{2}$ and is therefore not differentiable at $x=\\frac{1}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/state.json" "file_sha256" "c528af12e32c51bb13e2c6a2c8187de963ee2bc86d9d5b36574231eba23d0e7f"} {"question" "q-47686" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3fc0483942561db3ea45757c1e71d91075bb66bcb91621aa8b7a809a03e90641"} {"question" "q-47686" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/state.json" "file_sha256" "c528af12e32c51bb13e2c6a2c8187de963ee2bc86d9d5b36574231eba23d0e7f"} {"question" "q-47686" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/assets/1f9ccdd0ab4829b8f5fe53660c8d7a48dd5f85b90f979bc7d927e4a141d2e6c2.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/assets/2733ec295867c20225f4b5d75f5cbe4bfbc91d9b8f3739520bcf4ce75c4d185c.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/assets/3ec213a7183bd1a9ed039d185361581b4a687462a8bfd0e5f885090bf53bf09f.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/assets/6d8c3bcf0d95b358b601f55746b256d1edf936c88e0dc4bd6c119fdb45327150.png"] ["image" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/assets/a8b5f35bec07979fc1e0d1eb4de3e6ad369bbce48ec4e1943380b9bac2e9feee.png"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/state.json" "file_sha256" "c528af12e32c51bb13e2c6a2c8187de963ee2bc86d9d5b36574231eba23d0e7f"} {"question" "q-47686" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/image "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/assets/2733ec295867c20225f4b5d75f5cbe4bfbc91d9b8f3739520bcf4ce75c4d185c.png"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/state.json" "file_sha256" "c528af12e32c51bb13e2c6a2c8187de963ee2bc86d9d5b36574231eba23d0e7f"} {"question" "q-51072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function given below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}-x+4, & x<1 \\\\ 0, & x=1 \\\\ x+2, & x>1\\end{aligned}\\,\n$$\n\n- It is continuous and differentiable at $x=1$\n- It is differentiable on the interval $(-2,2)$\n- It is **not** differentiable at $x=1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/state.json" "file_sha256" "c528af12e32c51bb13e2c6a2c8187de963ee2bc86d9d5b36574231eba23d0e7f"} {"question" "q-51072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "- Statement I is false and statement III is true. Indeed,  let's investigate the continuity of $f(x).$ It is continuous everywhere except, possibly, $x=1.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}(-x+4)=-1+4=3 \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}(x+2)=1+2=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {1}^{+}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to {1}^{-}}f(x)\\ne f(1)=0,\n$$\n\nand so $f(x)$ is not continuous at $x=1$ (it has a removable discontinuity there). Hence, it is also not differentiable at this point.\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ it is not differentiable on any interval containing this point. Consequently, it is not differentiable on $(-2,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/state.json" "file_sha256" "c528af12e32c51bb13e2c6a2c8187de963ee2bc86d9d5b36574231eba23d0e7f"} {"question" "q-51072" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3fc0483942561db3ea45757c1e71d91075bb66bcb91621aa8b7a809a03e90641"} {"question" "q-51072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/state.json" "file_sha256" "c528af12e32c51bb13e2c6a2c8187de963ee2bc86d9d5b36574231eba23d0e7f"} {"question" "q-51072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/state.json" "file_sha256" "c528af12e32c51bb13e2c6a2c8187de963ee2bc86d9d5b36574231eba23d0e7f"} {"question" "q-51072" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/state.json" "file_sha256" "c528af12e32c51bb13e2c6a2c8187de963ee2bc86d9d5b36574231eba23d0e7f"} {"question" "q-101990" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/assets/e73fd08c7745ed049975a3a7c3af2f69e88bf0b374db741f96ce1812dc40f5f5.png)\n\nConsider the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. At which points is the function $f(x)$ **not** differentiable?" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/state.json" "file_sha256" "c528af12e32c51bb13e2c6a2c8187de963ee2bc86d9d5b36574231eba23d0e7f"} {"question" "q-101990" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The function is not differentiable wherever it has a discontinuity, corner, cusp, or vertical tangent.\n\n- At $x=1$ the function has a removable discontinuity, so it's not differentiable there.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/state.json" "file_sha256" "c528af12e32c51bb13e2c6a2c8187de963ee2bc86d9d5b36574231eba23d0e7f"} {"question" "q-101990" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3fc0483942561db3ea45757c1e71d91075bb66bcb91621aa8b7a809a03e90641"} {"question" "q-101990" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/state.json" "file_sha256" "c528af12e32c51bb13e2c6a2c8187de963ee2bc86d9d5b36574231eba23d0e7f"} {"question" "q-101990" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$x=0$  and $x=2$ only"] ["text" "$x=1$  and $x=2$ only"] ["text" "$x=1$ only"] ["text" "$x=2$ only"] ["text" "The function is differentiable at all points."]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/state.json" "file_sha256" "c528af12e32c51bb13e2c6a2c8187de963ee2bc86d9d5b36574231eba23d0e7f"} {"question" "q-101990" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$x=1$ only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/state.json" "file_sha256" "c528af12e32c51bb13e2c6a2c8187de963ee2bc86d9d5b36574231eba23d0e7f"} {"question" "q-101998" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following functions is differentiable at the point $x=0?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/state.json" "file_sha256" "c528af12e32c51bb13e2c6a2c8187de963ee2bc86d9d5b36574231eba23d0e7f"} {"question" "q-101998" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Notice that the function $q(x)$ is differentiable for all $x\\ne 1.$ Therefore, the correct answer is $q(x)={\\begin{aligned}x-1 & x<1 \\\\ x+1, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}.$\n\nAll other functions are not differentiable at $x=0.$\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is not defined at $x=0$, so it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $g(x)$ has a corner point at $x=0$, so it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $p(x)$ has a vertical tangent at  $x=0$, so it is not differentiable there.\n\n- The function $h(x)$ is not defined at $x=0$, so it is not differentiable there." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/state.json" "file_sha256" "c528af12e32c51bb13e2c6a2c8187de963ee2bc86d9d5b36574231eba23d0e7f"} {"question" "q-101998" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3fc0483942561db3ea45757c1e71d91075bb66bcb91621aa8b7a809a03e90641"} {"question" "q-101998" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/state.json" "file_sha256" "c528af12e32c51bb13e2c6a2c8187de963ee2bc86d9d5b36574231eba23d0e7f"} {"question" "q-101998" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "f(x)=\\frac{1}{x}"] ["math" "g(x)=-|x|"] ["math" "h(x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x"] ["math" "p(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x}"] ["math" "q(x)={\\begin{aligned}x-1 & x<1 \\\\ x+1, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/state.json" "file_sha256" "c528af12e32c51bb13e2c6a2c8187de963ee2bc86d9d5b36574231eba23d0e7f"} {"question" "q-101998" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "q(x)={\\begin{aligned}x-1 & x<1 \\\\ x+1, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14041401/state.json" "file_sha256" "c528af12e32c51bb13e2c6a2c8187de963ee2bc86d9d5b36574231eba23d0e7f"}] "usage" {} "basis" 1495 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
